  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🏡 Hobbit Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Shire names in Tolkien's style, from plain Bardo Kettlewell to a grand Took or a Buckland -doc."
    },
    "intro": "Tolkien's hobbits have some of the most comfortable names in fantasy: Bilbo, Frodo and Samwise, Baggins, Took and Gamgee. They follow clear patterns, and Tolkien explained several of them himself. This **hobbit name generator** builds new names in those patterns: plain Shire names, grand old Took names, Buckland names like Meriadoc's, and the flower and jewel names hobbits gave their daughters.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How hobbit names work",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Male names</strong> are often short and end in <em>-o</em>: Bilbo, Frodo, Drogo, Otho, Lotho. They sound homely and a bit old-fashioned.</li><li><strong>Female names</strong> are often flowers or jewels. In the appendices to <em>The Lord of the Rings</em>, Tolkien notes that hobbit girls were commonly given such names: Belladonna, Lobelia, Primula, Rose, Pearl.</li><li><strong>Family names</strong> are plain English-sounding words, often about the land or the home: Baggins (from Bag End), Proudfoot, Burrows, Hornblower, Brandybuck.</li></ul><p>All the names in this generator are new. None belong to a hobbit in Tolkien's books, so your character won't turn up in a family tree.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Tooks and Brandybucks",
        "content": "<p>Two great families break the pattern, and the generator's <strong>Family</strong> choice follows them:</p><ul><li><strong>The Tooks</strong> were the Shire's grandest and most adventurous family, and they gave their sons grand, old-sounding names: Bandobras, Fortinbras, Isengrim, Ferumbras. The <em>Took</em> option makes a new Took with a name like those.</li><li><strong>The Brandybucks</strong> of Buckland, across the Brandywine river, had names that sound different from the rest of the Shire: Meriadoc, Gorbadoc, Marmadoc, Saradoc, Rorimac. The <em>Brandybuck</em> option makes a new Brandybuck in that -doc and -mac style.</li></ul><p>A Took name says your hobbit comes from an old family with a taste for adventure; a Buckland name says they're from the edge of the Shire, by the river and the Old Forest.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Naming your own hobbit",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Match the family to the character.</strong> A cautious, respectable hobbit has a plain Shire name; a Took is expected to wander off.</li><li><strong>Use a nickname.</strong> Hobbits shorten names all the time: Peregrin is Pippin, Meriadoc is Merry, Samwise is Sam.</li><li><strong>Give the surname a place.</strong> Many Shire family names come from where the family lived: a hill, a burrow, a bank by the river.</li></ul><p>For D&amp;D's halflings, which began as a stand-in for hobbits, see the <a href=\"halfling-name-generator.html\">Halfling Name Generator</a>. For more Middle-earth, try the <a href=\"lotr-wizard-name-generator.html\">LOTR Wizard</a>, <a href=\"lotr-orc-name-generator.html\">LOTR Orc</a> and <a href=\"elf-name-generator.html\">Elf</a> generators.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Why do so many hobbit names end in -o?",
        "answer": "It's the commonest pattern for male hobbits in Tolkien's books (Bilbo, Frodo, Drogo, Otho), and gives them a homely, old-fashioned sound."
      },
      {
        "question": "How did hobbits name their daughters?",
        "answer": "Often after flowers or jewels, as Tolkien notes in the appendices to The Lord of the Rings: Belladonna, Lobelia, Primula, Rose, Pearl."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are these names from The Lord of the Rings?",
        "answer": "No. They're new names in Tolkien's patterns, chosen so none of them belongs to a hobbit in the books."
      }
    ]
  }
